        "topic": "Indices and Logarithms",
        "question_type": "Objective",
        "exam_year": "2002",
        "question_text": "<p>If <math>$\\log_{5}(x - 2) + \\log_{5}(x + 2) = 2$</math>, find the value of <math>$x$</math>.</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>Combine the logarithms using the product rule: <math>$\\log_{5}[(x - 2)(x + 2)] = 2$</math>.<br>This simplifies to <math>$\\log_{5}(x^2 - 4) = 2$</math>.<br>Convert to exponential form: <math>$x^2 - 4 = 5^2 = 25$</math>.<br>Solve for <math>$x$</math>: <math>$x^2 = 29$</math>, so <math>$x = \\pm \\sqrt{29}$</math>.<br>Since <math>$x - 2 > 0$</math> and <math>$x + 2 > 0$</math>, <math>$x = \\sqrt{29}$</math>.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
